Show a different interview state in the same native village workshop. A cream, clean field-guide panel occupies the right half. Left is a quiet EMPTY round, low-fenced meadow by the road in golden evening light. In midair above that empty plot only a modest, translucent dotted-ink planning sketch of a possible low circular arena with one small fighter and two groups of abstract enemy silhouettes. No constructed arena or crane. A little ivory tag beneath the sketch reads \"Idea sketch\". The confirmed real village and the unconfirmed idea should be easy to tell apart. Panel top \"Your idea\", followed by the player''s line exactly: \"an arena RPG with waves of enemies\". Then present the finding as the main headline \"No recipe fits yet\". Under it two readable short sentences: \"Enemies that fight back need new behaviour.\" and \"Expect a slower build, more risk and more builder questions.\" Put a simple line drawing of three overlapping blank recipe cards with a magnifying lens beside the finding. Use remaining panel space for an actionable small first decision, heading \"What starts the next wave?\" and a clearly labeled \"Foreman suggests\" choice \"When the arena is clear\", then one short reason \"A clear arena gives you a breather.\" Bottom primary honey button \"Keep planning\" and smaller secondary \"Revise idea\". At foot \"Draft · Nothing spent\". No danger-red fatal error, enemies in combat, false ready-to-build claim, game stats, or costume-fantasy interface. Keep it warm, honest and spacious.', [Text.UTF8Encoding]::new("'$false))
