#f2b230), terracotta signal accents, squared corners with small chamfers, offset ink shadows, confident wide humanist sans titles, condensed sans labels, crisp untextured typesetting. Use large readable lettering and restrained line icons. Strong hierarchy and breathing room even at 1280 × 720. Anchor upper left with a compact ivory nameplate bearing a tiny flag icon and the word \"Workshop\", plus a small back chevron. Do not add a game logo, window frame, invented microcopy, fake text or ornamental statistics. No website dashboard, desk photograph, sepia parchment or border around the image. The actual world has rolling spring-yellow green grass, sparse conical firs and simple polygon trees, low round timber fences, a winding ochre road, small red-roof plank cabins, blocky distant flat-topped mauve mesas under a peach-and-rose sunset. Low-poly flat toon faces, dark fine ink outlines, violet cast shadows, toy construction kit proportions. Native game UI is clean and sharp against the world. Follow the quoted text precisely; quotes delimit UI copy, don\u0027t print quote marks unless explicitly requested.\n\nDepict an active hockey build on Meadow plot. The whole upper and central scene is the live three-quarter view over a large round timber-fenced lot, entrance path to village road at the front, one big yellow lattice crane and three distinct low kit bays: rink base and red-edged ice boards in first bay, tiny testing skaters in the second, scaffold and unfinished blue stands in third. Late sun, warm grass, plum shadows, distant mesas; game screenshot, not a painting. Directly attached to the three bays put three little ivory signposts with two clearly readable lines each, reading \"Rink / Built\", \"Skaters / Checking\", \"Stands / On hold\" (slash denotes line break). Use check, magnifier, pause icons respectively for quick reading. Beside the Workshop nameplate, clear heading \"Meadow hockey\". Upper right an unobtrusive readout \"18 min elapsed\" and \"240k tokens spent\". Give the bottom about 34% of the screen to two clean well-spaced cream native surfaces. The left is a running brief headed \"Decided\" featuring \"3 a side · Computer opponents\" and \"3 periods · 36 seconds each\" and secondary action \"Request a change\". Add a small single site photograph thumbnail with simple white edge depicting rink boards being fitted, caption \"Latest picture\". The larger bottom-right surface is a Foreman message visibly tied to the held stands bay by a little ink leader or speech tail. A terracotta pause icon beside \"Stands crew is waiting\". Main question: \"Where should the stands go?\" Reason: \"The north side keeps the gate clear.\" A prominent honey action \"Use north side\" and quieter action \"Choose another side\". Make the crew\u0027s pending decision the strongest clickable thing. Do not block view of bays. Show only the requested text, lettering crisp enough to read the status signposts at game size."},{"n":"03-build-site","p":"Use case: ui-mockup. Draw ONE finished, full-bleed 16:9 in-game screenshot at 2048 × 1152. This is Third Life, a mouse-and-keyboard Godot game. All four screens use a village surveyor\u0027s field guide visual language: flat warm ivory enamel surfaces (#f3e8dc), nearly black plum ink (#2b2532), honey-yellow active controls (#f2b230), terracotta signal accents, squared corners with small chamfers, offset ink shadows, confident wide humanist sans titles, condensed sans labels, crisp untextured typesetting. Use large readable lettering and restrained line icons. Strong hierarchy and breathing room even at 1280 × 720. Anchor upper left with a compact ivory nameplate bearing a tiny flag icon and the word \"Workshop\", plus a small back chevron. Do not add a game logo, window frame, invented microcopy, fake text or ornamental statistics. No website dashboard, desk photograph, sepia parchment or border around the image. The actual world has rolling spring-yellow green grass, sparse conical firs and