ar.\" A prominent honey action \"Use north side\" and quieter action \"Choose another side\". Make the crew\u0027s pending decision the strongest clickable thing. Do not block view of bays. Show only the requested text, lettering crisp enough to read the status signposts at game size."},{"n":"04-arena-idea-try1","p":"Use case: ui-mockup. Draw ONE finished, full-bleed 16:9 in-game screenshot at 2048 × 1152. This is Third Life, a mouse-and-keyboard Godot game. All four screens use a village surveyor\u0027s field guide visual language: flat warm ivory enamel surfaces (#f3e8dc), nearly black plum ink (#2b2532), honey-yellow active controls (#f2b230), terracotta signal accents, squared corners with small chamfers, offset ink shadows, confident wide humanist sans titles, condensed sans labels, crisp untextured typesetting. Use large readable lettering and restrained line icons. Strong hierarchy and breathing room even at 1280 × 720. Anchor upper left with a compact ivory nameplate bearing a tiny flag icon and the word \"Workshop\", plus a small back chevron. Do not add a game logo, window frame, invented microcopy, fake text or ornamental statistics. No website dashboard, desk photograph, sepia parchment or border around the image. The actual world has rolling spring-yellow green grass, sparse conical firs and simple polygon trees, low round timber fences, a winding ochre road, small red-roof plank cabins, blocky distant flat-topped mauve mesas under a peach-and-rose sunset. Low-poly flat toon faces, dark fine ink outlines, violet cast shadows, toy construction kit proportions. Native game UI is clean and sharp against the world. Follow the quoted text precisely; quotes delimit UI copy, don\u0027t print quote marks unless explicitly requested.\n\nShow a different interview state in the same native village workshop. A cream, clean field-guide panel occupies the right half. Left is a quiet EMPTY round, low-fenced meadow by the road in golden evening light. In midair above t